100, proved by a volumetric argument: the set of 13-point configurations avoiding all triangles of area below 1/100 has Hausdorff dimension strictly less than 26 (the ambient dimension), hence is empty.", "probability": 0.05}, {"conjecture": "A metric entropy argument using a hexagonal tiling with 78 cells of equal area yields the upper bound 1/78: by pigeonhole three of the 13 points lie in cells whose centers form a triangle of area at most 1/78, and small cell diameter controls the perturbation.", "probability": 0.06}, {"conjecture": "The sharp upper bound is 1/72, obtained by a three-scale partition argument: partition [0,1]^2 into 12 equal rectangles, then sub-partition the rectangle containing the most points, and show the third-level pigeonhole forces a triangle of area at most 1/72.", "probability": 0.08}, {"conjecture": "The maximum minimum triangle area is at most 1/60, proved by a metric entropy argument on ordered triples: there are C(13,3)*3! = 1716 ordered triples, and a packing of the configuration space into 1716 cells forces at least one triple into a near-degenerate position with area at most 1/60.", "probability": 0.05}]}
